<section class="dev-tools dev-console" data-controller="console">
  <h1>skyBlip&nbsp;Go: Bluetooth console <span>&middot; a real device over Web Bluetooth,
    <a href="<%= briefing_simulator_path %>">the simulator</a> is next door</span></h1>

  <div class="layout">
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Link</h2>
      <div class="bar">
        <button id="connect">Connect</button>
        <button id="disconnect" disabled>Disconnect</button>
        <span class="state" id="state">idle</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hint">Chrome or Edge, on a page served from localhost or https. Safari and
        everything on iOS have no Web Bluetooth at all. The picker lists anything advertising
        Nordic UART or HM-10, so a SoftRF unit answers here too.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Serial stream</h2>
      <div class="bar">
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="follow" checked> follow</label>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="stamps" checked> timestamps</label>
        <button id="clearstream">Clear</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tape" id="stream">-</div>
      <form class="row" id="send">
        <input aria-label="NMEA sentence" type="text" id="line" placeholder="$PSRFC,?" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
        <button type="submit">Send</button>
      </form>
      <div class="hint">A sentence with no <code>*</code> gets its NMEA checksum appended, so
        <code>$PSRFC,?</code> leaves as <code>$PSRFC,?*47</code> and SoftRF answers with its
        settings. skyBlip reads nothing here: its write characteristic exists only because
        XCSoar refuses a UART service without one, and its own commands live in the config
        panel, which appears once a device carrying that service connects.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel" id="configpanel" hidden>
      <h2>Config (skyBlip)</h2>
      <div class="row" id="commands"></div>
      <div class="tape" id="replies">-</div>
      <form class="row" id="sendjson">
        <input aria-label="JSON command" type="text" id="json" placeholder='{"command":"set","callsign":"S-BLIP"}'
               autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
        <button type="submit">Write</button>
      </form>
      <div class="hint">Type a command as JSON with full names and byte strings in hex: it leaves as
        the CBOR frame <code>schemas/link.v1.json</code> numbers, and each reply shows the same way.
        The first session that writes claims config until it disconnects.
        A <code>set</code> is refused in flight and on a low cell, then staged: the device asks
        for a button press, and the reply lands here when you give it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

</section>
